From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A.Cervantez" <155030226+Ywach404@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:48:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: give the pad a dialogue mode with stick flicks for
 answers

The dialogue HUD (client CHudDialog) shows no cursor, keeps the pointer
relative and handles only the mouse wheel (history scroll), the left
button (history toggle) and the number keys 1 to 4, picking on
release. It has four answer slots and no arrow keys or highlight. So
dialogue never reached menu mode, and answers were only on the d-pad,
which the PSVR2 Sense pair does not have. Mouse aim cannot help here,
and stepping through answers would have nothing to show.

The pump now reads the HUD's own state each frame. The HUD stores
itself at client+0x3EE138, its vtable is checked so a stale pointer
cannot read as a dialogue, and the active flag at +0x1B4 is written
only by SetActive (the server's DialogCtrl) and Init. The game's
IsDialogActive reads the same pair. Changes are logged as "dialogue
open" and "dialogue closed".

While the flag is set and no cursor is shown, the pad is in dialogue
mode. A right stick flick sends 1 (up), 2 (right), 3 (down) or 4
(left), the d-pad's own layout. A flick fires past half tilt on the
axis that is further out, lets go under the dead zone, and does not
repeat. It is judged after both axes are read. A dialogue starts
unarmed, so a stick that was looking as it opened must centre before
it can pick. Cross stays space, which is +jump, the game's continue in
a dialogue. Circle is Escape, which the HUD ignores; only the server
closes a dialogue. Both sticks stop looking and walking, held
movement keys are lifted on entry, and a held flick's key is lifted
on exit. A shown cursor still wins, so a menu opened over a dialogue
works with the cursor as before. The d-pad keeps 1 to 4 everywhere.
controls.txt takes dialogue.<button>= overrides, including flickup,
flickright, flickdown and flickleft, and the log reports "pad mode
dialogue" and each first press in it.
